LND community investment and the long-term fiscal challenge
October 16, 2026  Reading time: 4 minutes
State of Loc Nation’s LND initiative connects investment in historically redlined and underfunded communities with business development, employment and measurable economic participation.
by Rev. Dr. Christina Clement, State of Loc Nation Global Public Benefit Corporation
The OECD’s October 9 article, “How large is the long-term fiscal challenge in OECD countries?”, identifies growing pressures from public debt, financing costs, population ageing and essential services. It also identifies an important opportunity: expanding employment can strengthen public finances by increasing the number of contributors relative to beneficiaries.
State of Loc Nation Global Public Benefit Corporation (SOLN GPBC) brings the Loc Nation Dollar (LND) initiative to this discussion. LND deployment focuses on historically redlined and underfunded communities, supporting their capacity to build businesses, create employment and participate more fully in economic activity.
SOLN GPBC is here and welcomes institutional collaboration. Communities that have experienced sustained underinvestment hold entrepreneurial talent, workers and productive capacity that can contribute to addressing long-term economic pressures.
Community investment can expand employment
The OECD article estimates that a reform-driven 10% increase in potential output achieved through higher employment could reduce the median long-run fiscal gap by approximately 2½ percentage points of GDP.
LND deployment can support this employment pathway through participating businesses and community programmes. Business funding can support equipment purchases, supplies, service expansion and hiring. Investment in transportation, childcare and training can help people enter and remain in employment.
Participating businesses accepting and spending LND can connect community purchasing power with local production. Where this generates additional employment and taxable business activity, it can broaden the contribution base supporting public services.
The OECD’s analysis provides a reason to examine this approach. LND’s specific contribution must be established through measured implementation.
