These figures are scenario assumptions, providing measurable targets rather than reporting completed results. Aggregate calculations use unrounded amounts.
If participating businesses create and sustain the assumed jobs, the scenario generates annual wages of approximately 933,333.33 LND, equivalent to $700 million USD, once full employment is achieved.
Under the illustrative 10% effective tax assumption, potential annual public revenue would be approximately 93,333.33 LND, equivalent to $70 million USD, subject to applicable tax treatment and actual collection. This does not assume that external tax authorities accept LND.
The declared parity supplies the scenario’s accounting basis. Deployment agreements must establish purchasing capacity, participating suppliers, acceptance terms and any necessary conversion arrangements.
Institutional structure supports delivery
SOLN GPBC’s official website publishes case-file listings addressing the establishment of governments, governmental departments and equivalent entities. These include Received 49, listed as “Establishment of all governments,” and Received 50, concerning governmental departments and equivalent entities.
This institutional work belongs alongside the LND deployment discussion. The parallel government offices being established within SOLN’s framework can provide designated points of responsibility for community participation, business onboarding, allocation administration and performance reporting.
An implementation MOU can identify the responsible offices, participating institutions, respective authorities and agreed responsibilities. It can also establish how partners coordinate business support, workforce development, treasury administration and evaluation.
Fiscal benefits should be measured over time
The OECD’s findings show why the source of growth matters. Expanding employment can increase production and household earnings while improving the balance between contributors and beneficiaries.
For LND deployment, reporting can begin with a baseline and continue at 6, 12, 24 and 36 months. Measures should include funding actually used, additional sustained jobs, verified payroll, business survival, business revenues, merchant acceptance, redemption performance and public costs.
Evaluation should distinguish activity attributable to the programme from activity that would have occurred without it. Any net fiscal assessment must account for programme expenses, public contributions, displaced activity and the timing of employment growth.
The scenario’s annual revenue estimate is therefore a measurable assumption to test. It is not a three-year cumulative total or an established net fiscal gain.
An invitation to collaborate
SOLN GPBC welcomes engagement with OECD governments, development institutions, researchers, employers and community business organisations interested in connecting community investment with measurable employment outcomes.
Interested organisations are invited to submit a Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) to info@stateoflocnation.com for review and consideration.
An MOU can identify the communities served, LND deployment scale, participating offices, funding and acceptance arrangements, responsibilities and evaluation standards.
Historically redlined and underfunded communities have a meaningful contribution to make through enterprise, employment and productive investment. SOLN GPBC welcomes partners ready to develop and measure that contribution.
